<v Daniel>Right. The quire is too big.</v>

151
00:17:26.125 --> 00:17:36.055
<v Maya>Exactly. But if you aggressively deduplicate the data set, you remove that redundancy. You thin out the quire. Suddenly, individual images might carry causal weight again.</v>

152
00:17:36.443 --> 00:17:44.690
<v Daniel>That is wild. The very thing companies do to stop the AI from copying might be the thing that actually allows us to prove they used our data.</v>

153
00:17:45.110 --> 00:17:53.677
<v Maya>Yeah. But ultimately, Diane Gifford make a critical distinction here. An absence of copying is not the same thing as an absence of attribution.</v>

154
00:17:53.937 --> 00:17:54.377
<v Daniel>What do you mean?</v>

155
00:17:54.798 --> 00:18:05.306
<v Maya>Well, a model can generate a completely novel image that doesn't look like anything in the data set. But because of the scale, that novel image is still fundamentally unattributable to any specific creator.</v>

156
00:18:05.795 --> 00:18:20.790
<v Daniel>Okay, so what does this all mean for you and me? Like, imagine you are an artist or photographer and you see an AI image that clearly mimics your style. Having proved that attribution physically, mathematically decays at scale, we really have to look</v>

157
00:18:20.810 --> 00:18:23.953
<v Daniel>at the real world stakes here. Because they are massive.</v>

158
00:18:24.317 --> 00:18:34.327
<v Maya>The stakes are deeply, deeply legal and ethical. Dye and Gifford state plainly in their conclusion that this structural unattributability provides what they call a refutation of access.</v>

159
00:18:34.467 --> 00:18:37.450
<v Daniel>A refutation of access. What does that actually mean in a courtroom?</v>

160
00:18:37.710 --> 00:18:49.141
<v Maya>In copyright law, proving infringement usually requires proving that the model had access to and fundamentally relied upon the copyrighted work to produce the infringing image. You have to prove causality.</v>

161
00:18:49.521 --> 00:19:04.109
<v Daniel>But the paper just proved causality disappears. Diane Giffords showed that attributability decays at around ten to the power of four or ten to the power of five images. Today's commercial models, they are training on billions of images.</v>

162
00:19:04.209 --> 00:19:05.610
<v Maya>Right. Ten to the power of nine.</v>

163
00:19:05.870 --> 00:19:20.839
<v Daniel>they're operating so far past the threshold of attribution decay that they may be structurally immune to the attribution that copyright infringement claims depend on. Like, you can't successfully sue for infringement if an MIT scientist can prove the model</v>

164
00:19:20.859 --> 00:19:24.981
<v Daniel>would have generated the exact same image, even if your art was never in the data set.</v>

165
00:19:25.242 --> 00:19:38.452
<v Maya>It's a huge legal shield. But we have to sit with the incredible tension here because this exact same property cuts the other way. It is a total double-edged sword. If scale destroys attribution, it also fundamentally preserves privacy.</v>

166
00:19:38.572 --> 00:19:39.673
<v Daniel>No, the privacy flip side.</v>

167
00:19:39.813 --> 00:19:52.042
<v Maya>Exactly. Think about medical data or personal photos. Because generated pictures of people are causally independent of the real people in the training data, scaling up these models perfectly preserves the privacy of the individuals in the data set.</v>

168
00:19:52.354 --> 00:19:58.816
<v Daniel>Because the model isn't leaking their identity. Their specific image has absolutely no causal bearing on the output.</v>

169
00:19:59.056 --> 00:20:09.320
<v Maya>Right. The scale of the choir masks the individual voice. Scale preserves privacy at the exact same time that it completely dissolves credit to artists.</v>

170
00:20:10.102 --> 00:20:23.749
<v Daniel>It is this profound structural trade-off. We gain the ability to build massive private systems that don't leak our personal data, but we lose the ability to give credit to the millions of human creators who built the foundation.</v>

171
00:20:24.249 --> 00:20:39.157
<v Maya>It forces us to entirely rethink how we interact with and govern these systems. If we can't trace a line back to a single image or a single artist, All our current frameworks for copyright and compensation are trying to solve a problem that the basic physics of the model has already rendered</v>

172
00:20:39.238 --> 00:20:39.698
<v Maya>obsolete.</v>

173
00:20:39.858 --> 00:20:54.848
<v Daniel>It really leaves you spinning. Thank you for joining us on this deep dive into the muddy, frankly, decaying waters of AI attribution. We started by asking what happens when you pull that digital thread connecting an AI image to its source. And we found out that at a certain scale, the thread just dissolves</v>

174
00:20:54.888 --> 00:20:55.448
<v Daniel>in your hands. It</v>

175
00:20:55.489 --> 00:20:56.089
<v Maya>just vanishes.</v>

176
00:20:56.209 --> 00:21:11.183
<v Daniel>Which leaves us with one final lingering question to mull over tonight. If the math proves that attribution to individual images, individual people, and individual artists is physically dissolving at scale, what course or unit could credit or liability possibly attach to?</v>

177
00:21:12.345 --> 00:21:17.457
<v Daniel>When you look at a generated masterpiece on your feet tomorrow morning, who is left to answer for a picture that no one in particular made?</v>
